experiment: slotwin
  SHORT RUN — 150000 ticks against a minimum of 900000. derived: §58. §53's EXACT arms, budget and genome -- 120 sessions -- with its
  primary scored over a PRE-REGISTERED WINDOW instead of the whole session.
  §53 read the derived index's slot term as +0.0081 +- 0.0170 against a
  pinned ceiling of +0.1293 and closed the expression route. §54 then refuted
  overwrite and left AVERAGING standing, which gives (1-2w) of ceiling -- and
  at the derived arm's MEASURED w = 0.1712 that is +0.0850, which §53's
  figure misses by 4.52 SE. That contradiction is already in the archive and
  has never been stated. §56 supplies the candidate reason: the derived index
  is THREE PHASES, degenerate in 22 and 18 of 24 creatures over the first two
  eighths and 16 of 24 in the last, but PERFECT in all 24 through e4-e7 at a
  quarter of the pooled error rate -- so §53's null was measured partly over
  windows in which there was NO INDEX AT ALL. In CLEAN (w 0.0326) averaging
  predicts +0.1209, 93.5% of the ceiling, so the two models sit 5.02 SE apart
  and §53's own arm sizes resolve them. PRIMARY: the SLOT TERM, which is
  object-conditioned BY CONSTRUCTION since (n_0s - n_1s) is the label
  difference -- not a margin (§57's defect). Five-way, every boundary 2 SE
  from one of the two competing models, plus a sixth branch that REFUSES when
  they sit closer than 4 SE. Clause 0 rebuilds cell_g/cell_n from the new
  per-probe vector and must reproduce §53's published table to four decimals
  INCLUDING the SE; clause 1 re-earns the pinned residual == 0 in every
  eighth; clause 2 puts the vacuity gate on the arm that is KNOWN to express,
  because the question is whether a 17-probe window can carry a slot term at
  all; clause 3 TESTS the phase cut's transfer from §56's pin_mode 5 arm
  rather than assuming it. No core change, no new genome field, no RNG draw,
  so the pinned hash cannot move. kRead: every branch prints
  Everything below is printed for debugging and is NOT a
  measurement; this run cannot pass whatever it reads.

  the question      §58. §53 read the derived index's slot term as a null
                    POOLED OVER THE WHOLE SESSION. §56 then showed that
                    index is degenerate in 22 and 18 of 24 creatures in the
                    first two eighths and 16 of 24 in the last, and PERFECT
                    in all 24 through e4-e7. Does it express THERE?
  the contradiction §54 left AVERAGING standing: (1-2w) of ceiling. At the
                    derived arm's measured w = 0.1712 that is +0.0850, and §53
                    measured +0.0081 +- 0.0170 -- short by 4.53 SE. Already in
                    the archive, never stated.
  the method        §53's EXACT primary -- the SLOT TERM, where (n_0s - n_1s)
                    IS the label difference, so it is a DEPENDENCE and not a
                    magnitude -- scored over a PRE-REGISTERED window. One
                    per-probe vector added; no core change, no RNG draw.
  the two models    §53's NULL: 0.    AVERAGING in CLEAN (w 0.0326): +0.1209,
                    which is 93.5% of the pinned ceiling +0.1293.
  session           150.0 s of simulated life x 120 sessions, 4 arms
  instrument        slotwin   trial seed 0000000001357f69   120 sessions
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)

